From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] grt: global_route -backing_file backs the routing grid with a
 file
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A large design's routing grid can need more memory than the machine has:
on a 34 M GCell x 9 layer die global route peaks well above 100 GB, and
it runs only where swap was set up for it, failing hours in where it
was not.

global_route -backing_file <file> reserves a file for the grid's 2D and
3D edges and its capacity per layer when the grid is set up, and maps
those arrays there; the kernel then pages them to the file instead of to
swap. The reservation (posix_fallocate) fails at once when the disk
cannot hold the grid, and the file is unlinked as soon as it exists, so
it leaves nothing behind. The arrays keep their addresses and contents:
the routes are the same as without it. Without -backing_file nothing
changes.

gcd_backing_file routes gcd with a backing file and expects gcd's guides.

Carried in bazel-orfs for OpenROAD#12075, not upstreamed; retires with
the //:bump onto an OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
